=== L2 Reading Skew, Outliers and Distributions ===
id: 3ebf8d9e-f516-42af-b1fd-63d9ee0a13ce
slug: reading-skew-outliers-and-distributions
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:29:10.001503+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 41

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Identifying positive and negative skew, commenting on outliers, and recognising binomial and Normal distributions.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is a Distribution?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">When you collect a set of data, the values spread across a range. The way those values are spread &mdash; which values are common, which are rare, whether the data clusters symmetrically or leans to one side &mdash; is the <dfn class="term" data-def="The pattern of how values in a dataset are spread, showing which values occur frequently and which occur rarely.">distribution</dfn> of the data. Reading the shape of a distribution is a core interpretation skill: it tells you whether the data is symmetric, skewed, or has unusual values at the extremes.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Symmetrical Distributions</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A distribution where the left and right sides are roughly mirror images of each other, with the mean, median and mode all at the centre.">symmetrical distribution</dfn> is balanced &mdash; the left and right sides are roughly mirror images of each other. In a perfectly symmetrical distribution, the mean, median, and mode are all equal and sit at the centre. A classic symmetrical shape is the bell curve, which you will encounter later in this lesson when looking at the Normal distribution.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Positive Skew</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A distribution with a longer tail on the right-hand side, where most values are clustered at the lower end and a few high values pull the mean above the median.">positively skewed distribution</dfn> has a longer tail on the right. Most values cluster at the lower end of the scale, with a few unusually high values stretching the tail to the right. Because those extreme high values pull the mean upwards, the mean is greater than the median in a positively skewed distribution.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n7">A real-world example: household income in the UK is positively skewed. Most households earn in a moderate range, but a small number of very high earners stretch the tail. The mean income is pulled upwards by these high earners, making it higher than the median. This is why the median is often a more representative average for income data.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Negative Skew</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A distribution with a longer tail on the left-hand side, where most values are clustered at the higher end and a few low values pull the mean below the median.">negatively skewed distribution</dfn> has a longer tail on the left. Most values cluster at the higher end of the scale, with a few unusually low values stretching the tail to the left. The mean is pulled downwards by those extreme low values, making it less than the median.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n10">A real-world example: scores on an easy test might show negative skew. Most students score highly (the cluster is on the right), but a small number of students who struggled pull the tail to the left. The mean score would be lower than the median because of those very low scores.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write from memory: for a positively skewed distribution, which is larger &mdash; the mean or the median? For a negatively skewed distribution? What does each tell you about the shape of the tail?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Positive skew: tail on the right, mean &gt; median. Negative skew: tail on the left, mean &lt; median. A useful memory trick: the skew is named after the direction of the TAIL, not the main cluster.</p>
</div>


<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11b" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write from memory: what is the relationship between mean and median in positively skewed, negatively skewed, and symmetrical distributions?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Positive skew: tail on the right, mean &gt; median. Negative skew: tail on the left, mean &lt; median. Symmetrical: mean = median. Remember: the skew is named after the direction of the TAIL, not where the main cluster sits.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Commenting on Outliers</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">An <dfn class="term" data-def="A data value that lies far away from the main cluster of values in a dataset.">outlier</dfn> is a value that stands apart from the rest of the data &mdash; either much larger or much smaller than typical. Outliers can arise from genuine extreme values (a student who scored 100% on a test where everyone else scored between 40% and 80%), measurement errors (a data entry typo turning 160 cm into 16 cm), or a genuinely different subgroup in the data.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n14">When commenting on outliers in exam questions, you should: (1) identify the outlier by referring to its approximate value, (2) suggest whether it might be a genuine extreme value or a possible error, and (3) comment on its effect on any averages you have calculated. An outlier pulls the mean towards it but does not affect the median.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: Confusing Skew Direction with the Cluster Location</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Students often say &ldquo;positive skew has most values on the positive side of the graph.&rdquo; This is wrong. Positive skew is named after the direction of the TAIL, not where the main cluster sits. In a positively skewed distribution, the TAIL goes right (in the positive direction) and the CLUSTER sits at the lower end.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">A quick check: if someone earned the most money in a room, are they pulling the mean income up? Yes &mdash; so income data is likely positively skewed (long right tail, even though most people cluster at lower incomes). That is positive skew.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="higher-only">
  <h2 data-narration-id="n17">Binomial Distribution (Higher)</h2>
  <p data-narration-id="n18">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A probability distribution that arises when counting the number of successes in a fixed number of independent trials, each with the same probability of success.">binomial distribution</dfn> describes the number of successes in a fixed number of independent trials, where each trial has the same probability of success. The word &ldquo;binomial&rdquo; will appear in your exam. The key characteristics are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li data-narration-id="n19">A fixed number of trials (e.g. flipping a coin 5 times).</li>
    <li data-narration-id="n20">Each trial has only two possible outcomes (success or failure).</li>
    <li data-narration-id="n21">The probability of success is constant across all trials.</li>
    <li data-narration-id="n22">The trials are independent of each other.</li>
  </ul>
  <p data-narration-id="n23">In your exam, the number of trials \(n\) will be no greater than 5. The distribution is symmetric when the probability of success is 0.5, and skewed when the probability is not 0.5. You do not need to use formal notation for the binomial distribution &mdash; describing its characteristics in words is sufficient.</p>

  <h2 data-narration-id="n24">The Normal Distribution and the 68/95 Rule (Higher)</h2>
  <p data-narration-id="n25">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A symmetric, bell-shaped probability distribution where most values cluster near the mean and become increasingly rare further from the mean.">Normal distribution</dfn> is a symmetric, bell-shaped distribution where values cluster around the mean and become progressively rarer further away. It describes many real-world characteristics: the heights of adults, birth weights, exam scores, and measurement errors all tend to follow approximately Normal distributions.</p>
  <p data-narration-id="n26">The specification requires you to know two key results about the Normal distribution:</p>
  <ul>
    <li data-narration-id="n27">Approximately <strong>68%</strong> of the data lies within one standard deviation of the mean.</li>
    <li data-narration-id="n28">Approximately <strong>95%</strong> of the data lies within two standard deviations of the mean.</li>
    <li data-narration-id="n29">Values more than <strong>three standard deviations</strong> from the mean are very unusual.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 data-narration-id="n30">Action and Warning Lines in Quality Assurance (Higher)</h2>
  <p data-narration-id="n31">In manufacturing and quality control, products are sampled regularly to check they meet a standard. The Normal distribution is used to set <dfn class="term" data-def="Lines on a quality control chart set at two standard deviations from the target mean; a value outside these lines suggests the process may need monitoring.">warning lines</dfn> (at two standard deviations from the target) and <dfn class="term" data-def="Lines on a quality control chart set at three standard deviations from the target mean; a value outside these lines indicates the process should be stopped and investigated.">action lines</dfn> (at three standard deviations from the target). If a sample mean falls outside the action lines, the production process is stopped and investigated, because such a result would be very unusual if the process were running correctly.</p>

  <h2 data-narration-id="n32">Standardising Data (Higher)</h2>
  <p data-narration-id="n33">To compare values from two different distributions (e.g. a student's score in Maths and their score in English, which have different means and standard deviations), you can standardise by converting each value to a z-score: $$z = \frac{x - \bar{x}}{s}$$ where \(x\) is the value, \(\bar{x}\) is the mean, and \(s\) is the standard deviation. The formula will be given in the question. A z-score tells you how many standard deviations above or below the mean a value lies, allowing fair comparison across different scales.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Applying the 68/95 Rule: A Worked Example</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n34">The heights of a group of adults are Normally distributed with a mean of 170 cm and a standard deviation of 8 cm.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n35">Within one standard deviation (162 cm to 178 cm): approximately 68% of adults. Within two standard deviations (154 cm to 186 cm): approximately 95% of adults. Heights above 194 cm or below 146 cm (three standard deviations away) are very unusual.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n37">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n38">Positive skew has a longer tail on the right (mean &gt; median); negative skew has a longer tail on the left (mean &lt; median); a symmetrical distribution has mean = median. The skew is named for the direction of the tail.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n39">Outliers stand far apart from the main cluster, may be genuine extreme values or data errors, and pull the mean towards them without affecting the median.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n40">Higher: the Normal distribution follows the 68/95 rule (68% within one standard deviation, 95% within two). Values more than three standard deviations from the mean are very unusual &mdash; this forms the basis of action and warning lines in quality control.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n36">On questions asking you to describe or identify skew, always name the direction (&ldquo;positive skew&rdquo; or &ldquo;negative skew&rdquo;) AND describe the tail direction (&ldquo;the tail stretches to the right&rdquo;). For Higher questions about the Normal distribution and the 68/95 rule, show your working clearly: write out the calculation \(	ext{mean} \pm 2 	imes 	ext{standard deviation}\) before stating the range. On outlier questions, always comment on its effect on the mean compared to the median &mdash; this is frequently the second mark in a two-mark question.</p>

