 {
  "text": "The ages at which a group of people bought their first car are recorded. The distribution is positively skewed.\nWhich of the following statements about the mean and median is correct?\nA) Mean < Median\nB) Mean = Median\nC) Mean > Median\nD) The relationship between mean and median cannot be determined from skew alone.",
  "type": "1 mark — Multiple Choice",
  "marks": "C [1 mark] — In a positively skewed distribution, the long right tail pulls the mean above the median."
 },
 {
  "text": "A dataset of 15 students' test scores is: 45, 48, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56, 58, 59, 60, 61, 62, 95.\nThe score of 95 appears to be an outlier.\nWork out the mean and median of the dataset, and comment on the effect of the outlier on each.",
  "type": "2 marks — Work Out",
  "marks": "Mean = (sum of all values) / 15. Sum = 45+48+50+51+52+53+54+55+56+58+59+60+61+62+95 = 859. Mean = 859/15 = 57.27 (accept 57.3) [1 mark].\nMedian = 8th value when ordered = 55 [1 mark].\nComment (for context): the outlier of 95 pulls the mean upwards (from approximately 55 to 57.3) but does not affect the median."
 },
 {
  "text": "A student looks at the following description of a distribution: 'Most values are clustered at the higher end of the scale, with a few very low values stretching the tail in the opposite direction.'\nDescribe the shape of this distribution, including the direction of the skew and the relative positions of the mean and median.",
  "type": "3 marks — Describe",
  "marks": "The distribution is negatively skewed [1].\nThe tail stretches to the left [1].\nThe mean is less than the median because the few low values pull the mean downwards [1]."
 },
 {
  "text": "A production line fills packets of crisps with a target weight of 150 g. The weights are Normally distributed with a standard deviation of 4 g.\nCalculate the range within which approximately 95% of packet weights will fall, and explain the significance of a packet weighing 162 g.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Mastering: Calculation correct and explanation developed; Secure: Calculation correct with partial explanation.\n95% range: mean +/- 2 standard deviations = 150 +/- 8 g = 142 g to 158 g [2 marks].\n162 g is more than 3 standard deviations above the mean (150 + 3*4 = 162 g exactly) [1].\nThis is very unusual for a correctly running process and would trigger an investigation; it would fall outside the action line [1]."
 },
 {
  "text": "A factory sets warning lines at two standard deviations from the target mean and action lines at three standard deviations. Samples are taken hourly.\nSuggest and justify what the factory quality manager should do if three consecutive sample means fall between the warning line and the action line.",
  "type": "5 marks — Suggest and Justify",
  "marks": "Award 1 mark per suggestion and 1 mark per justification, up to 5 marks.\nMastering: A specific action and a justified reason linked to the statistical meaning of the warning/action lines.\n- Monitor the process more closely or increase the sampling frequency [1] because three consecutive means between the warning and action lines suggests a trend away from the target, even though no individual result has crossed the action line [1].\n- Investigate possible causes of drift (e.g. machine calibration, raw material variability) [1] before the means cross the action line and production needs to be stopped [1].\n- Record and document the values so that patterns can be identified [1]; a sustained pattern of values above the warning line (even if none crosses the action line) is a known warning sign in quality control practice [1].\nSecure: One specific action with a justified reason.\nDeveloping: Names an action without justification.\nEmerging: States 'do something' without specifying what."
 },
 {
  "text": "The scores in a maths test have a mean of 62 and a standard deviation of 12. The scores in an English test have a mean of 55 and a standard deviation of 8. A student scores 74 in maths and 71 in English.\nDiscuss in which subject the student performed better relative to their peers, using standardised scores in your answer.",
  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Award up to 6 marks for a calculation, interpretation, comparison, and judgement.\nMastering: Both z-scores calculated correctly, interpreted, compared, and a judgement made.\n- Maths z-score: (74 - 62) / 12 = 12/12 = 1.0 [1]. This means the maths score is 1 standard deviation above the mean [1].\n- English z-score: (71 - 55) / 8 = 16/8 = 2.0 [1]. This means the English score is 2 standard deviations above the mean [1].\n- Comparison: the student performed better relative to peers in English (z = 2.0) than in maths (z = 1.0), despite scoring lower in absolute terms [1].\n- Judgement: the standardised score accounts for the different difficulty levels and spread of scores in each subject, making it a fairer comparison than comparing raw marks [1].\nSecure: Both z-scores correct with interpretation.\nDeveloping: One z-score calculated with partial interpretation.\nEmerging: Compares raw scores only without standardising."
 }
]

