=== L3 Time Series, Trends and Rates of Change ===
id: 574e2290-cca8-43a2-b76c-40f72ed77937
slug: time-series-trends-and-rates-of-change
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:33:10.085468+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 38

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Spotting trends and seasonal patterns over time, and using rates of change and index numbers in real contexts.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is a Time Series?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A sequence of data values recorded at successive points in time, used to identify trends, seasonal patterns, and changes over time.">time series</dfn> is data recorded at successive time points &mdash; monthly sales figures, quarterly unemployment rates, annual birth rates. Plotting these values against time produces a time series graph (sometimes called a line chart) that lets you see patterns that would be invisible in a table of numbers.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Trends</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The overall long-term direction of a time series, ignoring short-term fluctuations.">trend</dfn> is the overall direction a time series is heading in the long run, ignoring short-term ups and downs. A trend might be rising (e.g. average house prices in the UK over decades), falling (e.g. infant mortality rates over the last century), or roughly constant. Identifying the trend is the first step in interpreting a time series.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n5">It is important to distinguish the trend from short-term variation. A shop&rsquo;s weekly sales might spike before Christmas and dip in January, but the underlying trend over several years might be consistently rising. The trend is what you would see if you smoothed out the seasonal bumps.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write from memory: what is the difference between a trend and a seasonal pattern? Give one real-world example of each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A trend is the long-term overall direction. A seasonal pattern is a regular, repeating short-term fluctuation at the same time each year (or each cycle). Both can be present in the same time series.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Seasonal and Cyclic Trends</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A regular, repeating pattern in a time series that occurs at the same time of year (or same stage of a regular cycle) every year.">seasonal trend</dfn> is a regular, repeating pattern tied to the time of year. Ice cream sales peak in summer and fall in winter every year; gas and electricity consumption peaks in winter every year; retail sales peak before Christmas every year. These patterns are predictable and expected.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n9">A cyclic trend is similar but not tied to a fixed calendar period &mdash; it represents a pattern that rises and falls over a longer, less regular time scale (such as economic business cycles that might last several years). The specification uses the term &ldquo;cyclic&rdquo; as well as &ldquo;seasonal&rdquo; and you should be able to interpret both.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n10">When a question asks you to use a seasonal or cyclic trend to make a prediction, always say: &ldquo;Based on the seasonal pattern in the data, I would expect [variable] to [rise/fall] in [period] because [reason from the pattern].&rdquo; Predictions based on time series should acknowledge that the pattern may not continue indefinitely.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Rates of Change</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A measure of how much a quantity changes per unit of time or per unit of population, calculated from a given formula.">rate of change</dfn> expresses how quickly something changes over time. Rather than just showing the raw count, a rate puts change in proportion &mdash; allowing meaningful comparison between populations of different sizes or time periods of different lengths.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n13">The specification covers several specific rate measures used in real UK contexts. These include the birth rate (number of live births per 1,000 people per year), the death rate (number of deaths per 1,000 people per year), house price change, and unemployment rates. All rate-of-change formulae will be given in the question &mdash; you will not be asked to recall them from memory. What matters is reading the formula carefully and applying it correctly to the data given.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: Confusing the Rate with the Count</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">A larger country has more births per year than a smaller one, simply because it has more people. Comparing raw birth counts tells you little. The birth rate (births per 1,000 population) corrects for population size, allowing a fair comparison between countries or regions of different sizes.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Similarly, a rise in the number of deaths does not necessarily mean mortality is worsening &mdash; the population may have grown or aged. The death rate puts this in proportion. Always use rates when comparing populations of different sizes.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Index Numbers</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17"><dfn class="term" data-def="A number that expresses the value of a quantity relative to a base period value, set to 100, enabling comparison over time.">Index numbers</dfn> express a value relative to a chosen base period (usually set to 100). Instead of quoting actual prices or quantities, an index shows how much a value has changed from the base. If the base year has an index of 100 and a later year has an index of 115, then the value has risen by 15% from the base.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n18">UK economists and statisticians use three important index numbers that you should know by name:</p>

<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n19"><strong>RPI (Retail Price Index):</strong> Measures changes in the average price of a basket of goods and services purchased by households. Published monthly by the ONS. Used for uprating certain benefits, wages, and rail fares.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20"><strong>CPI (Consumer Price Index):</strong> Similar to RPI but uses a different methodology and basket of goods. The Bank of England uses CPI as its target measure for inflation.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21"><strong>GDP (Gross Domestic Product):</strong> Measures the total economic output of a country. GDP index tracks growth or contraction of the economy over time.</li>
</ul>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n22" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what do RPI, CPI, and GDP stand for? For each, write one sentence describing what it measures.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>RPI and CPI both measure price inflation, with RPI and CPI differing in methodology. GDP measures total economic output. All three are published by UK government bodies and used in policy decisions. All rate-of-change formulae are given in the exam &mdash; focus on reading and applying them, not memorising them.</p>
</div>

<div class="higher-only">
  <h2 data-narration-id="n23">Calculating Rates of Change (Higher)</h2>
  <p data-narration-id="n24">At Higher tier, you will be asked to calculate rates of change from tables using a formula that is given in the question. Typical examples include: calculating a birth rate from a table of births and population figures, or calculating a percentage change in house price from year-to-year data.</p>
  <p data-narration-id="n25">The key skill is identifying the right values to substitute into the formula. Read the column headers carefully to identify which values go where. State your working clearly and give your answer in the correct units (rates are usually expressed per 1,000 or as a percentage).</p>

  <h2 data-narration-id="n26">Weighted Index Numbers (Higher)</h2>
  <p data-narration-id="n27">A <dfn class="term" data-def="An index number that gives different items in the basket different weights according to their importance in typical household spending.">weighted index number</dfn> assigns different weights to different items based on their importance in a typical household&rsquo;s spending. For example, if a family spends 40% of its budget on housing and 5% on entertainment, housing gets a much larger weight in the index. A simple (unweighted) average of all price changes would understate the effect of rising housing costs on the household&rsquo;s total bill.</p>
  <p data-narration-id="n28">The CPI and RPI are both weighted indices. The formula for a weighted index will be given in the question. Your task is to apply it correctly: multiply each item&rsquo;s price relative by its weight, sum these products, and divide by the sum of the weights.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Interpreting a Time Series Graph: What to Look For</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n29">When asked to interpret a time series graph in the exam, work through these steps:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n30">State the overall trend &mdash; is it rising, falling, or steady over the period shown?</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n31">Identify any seasonal pattern &mdash; is there a regular peak and trough that repeats each year or cycle?</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n32">Note any unusual features &mdash; a sudden spike or dip that breaks the trend; is there a plausible real-world explanation?</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n33">Quantify where possible &mdash; &ldquo;the value rose from approximately 40 to 65 between 2010 and 2020&rdquo; is better than &ldquo;it went up.&rdquo;</li>
    </ul>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n35">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n36">A time series has a trend (the long-term direction) and may also have a seasonal pattern (a regular, repeating fluctuation). Both must be described when interpreting a time series graph, with specific values where possible.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n37">Rates of change (birth rate, death rate, house price change) put raw counts in proportion for fair comparison. All formulae are given in the question &mdash; focus on reading and applying them correctly.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n38">Index numbers (RPI, CPI, GDP) express values relative to a base period (= 100). Weighted index numbers assign different weights to items based on their importance in typical spending &mdash; unweighted averages would overstate or understate the impact of price changes on household budgets.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n34">On &lsquo;Describe&rsquo; questions about time series graphs, the most common error is describing what the graph looks like (&ldquo;the line goes up and down&rdquo;) rather than what it shows about the data (&ldquo;sales rose steadily from around 120,000 to 180,000 units between 2015 and 2020, with a seasonal dip each January&rdquo;). Always include the trend direction, specific values or time periods, and any seasonal pattern. On rate-of-change calculation questions, read the formula in the question carefully before substituting values &mdash; do not guess the formula from memory.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
