 succeeded in 215ms:
<h2 data-narration-id="n24">Interpreting Correlation Coefficients</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n25">At Foundation and Higher tier, you need to interpret a given <dfn class="term" data-def="A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of the relationship between the rank orders of two variables; denoted r_s.">Spearman's rank correlation coefficient</dfn>. It measures the strength and direction of correlation between the rank orders of two variables &mdash; not the raw values. It works for any data that can be ranked, including ordinal data.</p>
<div class="higher-only">
  <h2 data-narration-id="n26">Pearson's PMCC (Higher)</h2>
  <p data-narration-id="n26"><dfn class="term" data-def="A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables; denoted r.">Pearson's product moment correlation coefficient</dfn> (PMCC) measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables. The key distinction: Spearman's measures correlation of rank orders, whereas Pearson's measures the linear relationship itself. For a straight-line relationship between continuous variables, Pearson's is more appropriate; for ranked or ordinal data, Spearman's is preferred.</p>
</div>
<p data-narration-id="n27">For questions that use Spearman's rank formula, it will be given. You do not need to memorise it. Interpret the resulting value using the same thresholds above: \(|r_s| \geq 0.6\) = strong, \(0.2 \leq |r_s| < 0.6\) = weak, \(|r_s| < 0.2\) = no correlation.</p>

