+    "expect": "Award 1 mark per reason and 1 mark per justification, up to 5 marks.\nMastering: Two reasons, each with a justification linking to a specific feature of time series extrapolation.\n- The past trend may not continue [1]; economic conditions (interest rates, housing policy, global events) can shift the trend direction in ways that are impossible to predict 10 years ahead, so the extrapolated line may diverge significantly from actual prices [1].\n- Seasonal effects compound over time [1]; even small seasonal fluctuations accumulate over 10 years of extrapolation, making point estimates increasingly unreliable as the prediction horizon extends [1].\n- The time series itself may contain outliers or one-off events (e.g. a financial crash) [1]; if such events influenced the trend line used for extrapolation, the forecast inherits these distortions [1].\nSecure: Two reasons, one justified.\nDeveloping: One reason with a justification.\nEmerging: States 'the future is uncertain' without specifying why the time series makes it unreliable.",
+    "value": "Award 1 mark per reason and 1 mark per justification, up to 5 marks.\nMastering: Two reasons, each with a justification linking to a specific feature of time series extrapolation.\n- The past trend may not continue [1]; economic conditions (interest rates, housing policy, global events) can shift the trend direction in ways that are impossible to predict 10 years ahead, so the extrapolated line may diverge significantly from actual prices [1].\n- Seasonal patterns may change [1]; a pattern that has repeated in the past may alter, so a 10-year forecast may not match future prices [1].\n- The time series itself may contain outliers or one-off events (e.g. a financial crash) [1]; if such events influenced the trend line used for extrapolation, the forecast inherits these distortions [1].\nSecure: Two reasons, one justified.\nDeveloping: One reason with a justification.\nEmerging: States 'the future is uncertain' without specifying why the time series makes it unreliable.",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F6: Seasonal effects repeat or change; they do not automatically accumulate."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[5].marks",
+    "expect": "Award up to 6 marks for a balanced discussion with examples and a judgement.\nMastering: Arguments for using rates, acknowledgement of limitations, and a judgement.\n- Comparing total births is misleading because Country B will always have far more births simply because it has 30 times more people [1+1].\n- Birth rates (births per 1,000 population) control for population size, allowing a fairer comparison of the actual fertility patterns in each country [1+1].\n- Rates are more useful for policy purposes: a government wants to know whether the share of new births is declining relative to population size, not just the absolute number [1+1].\n- Limitation: birth rates do not capture age structure differences — a country with a very young population will have a higher birth rate even at the same fertility per woman [1].\n- Judgement: comparing birth rates is generally more meaningful than raw counts when populations differ in size, but further context (age structure, immigration) is still needed for full interpretation [1].\nSecure: Two threads developed.\nDeveloping: One thread with a relevant example.\nEmerging: States 'use a rate' without explaining why.",
+    "value": "Award up to 6 marks for a balanced discussion with examples and a judgement.\nMastering: Arguments for using rates, acknowledgement of limitations, and a judgement.\n- Comparing total births can be misleading because Country B may have more births simply because it has 30 times more people [1+1].\n- Birth rates (births per 1,000 population) control for population size, allowing a fairer comparison of the actual fertility patterns in each country [1+1].\n- Rates are more useful for policy purposes: a government wants to know whether the share of new births is declining relative to population size, not just the absolute number [1+1].\n- Limitation: birth rates do not capture age structure differences — a country with a very young population will have a higher birth rate even at the same fertility per woman [1].\n- Judgement: comparing birth rates is generally more meaningful than raw counts when populations differ in size, but further context (age structure, immigration) is still needed for full interpretation [1].\nSecure: Two threads developed.\nDeveloping: One thread with a relevant example.\nEmerging: States 'use a rate' without explaining why.",
