+[
+  {
+    "lesson": 1,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<div class=\"higher-only\">\n  <h2 data-narration-id=\"n24\">Interpreting Spearman's Rank and Pearson's PMCC (Higher)</h2>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n25\">At Higher tier, you need to interpret calculated values of two correlation coefficients. <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of the relationship between the rank orders of two variables; denoted r_s.\">Spearman's rank correlation coefficient</dfn> measures the strength and direction of correlation between the rank orders of two variables &mdash; not the raw values. It works for any data that can be ranked, including ordinal data.</p>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n26\"><dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables; denoted r.\">Pearson's product moment correlation coefficient</dfn> (PMCC) measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables. The key distinction: Spearman's measures correlation of rank orders, whereas Pearson's measures the linear relationship itself. For a straight-line relationship between continuous variables, Pearson's is more appropriate; for ranked or ordinal data, Spearman's is preferred.</p>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n27\">The formula for Spearman's rank will always be given in the question &mdash; you do not need to memorise it. What you do need to do is interpret the resulting value using the same thresholds above: \\(|r_s| \\geq 0.6\\) = strong, \\(0.2 \\leq |r_s| < 0.6\\) = weak, \\(|r_s| < 0.2\\) = no correlation.</p>\n</div>",
+    "replace": "<h2 data-narration-id=\"n24\">Interpreting Correlation Coefficients</h2>\n<p data-narration-id=\"n25\">At Foundation and Higher tier, you need to interpret a given <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of the relationship between the rank orders of two variables; denoted r_s.\">Spearman's rank correlation coefficient</dfn>. It measures the strength and direction of correlation between the rank orders of two variables &mdash; not the raw values. It works for any data that can be ranked, including ordinal data.</p>\n<div class=\"higher-only\">\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n26\"><strong>Pearson's PMCC (Higher):</strong> <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables; denoted r.\">Pearson's product moment correlation coefficient</dfn> (PMCC) measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables. The key distinction: Spearman's measures correlation of rank orders, whereas Pearson's measures the linear relationship itself. For a straight-line relationship between continuous variables, Pearson's is more appropriate; for ranked or ordinal data, Spearman's is preferred.</p>\n</div>\n<p data-narration-id=\"n27\">For questions that use Spearman's rank formula, it will be given. You do not need to memorise it. Interpret the resulting value using the same thresholds above: \\(|r_s| \\geq 0.6\\) = strong, \\(0.2 \\leq |r_s| < 0.6\\) = weak, \\(|r_s| < 0.2\\) = no correlation.</p>",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F1: Given Spearman interpretation is Additional Foundation content."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 1,
+    "field": "flashcard_questions",
+    "path": "[9].q",
+    "expect": "What does Spearman's rank correlation coefficient measure? (Higher)",
+    "value": "What does Spearman's rank correlation coefficient measure?",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F1: Given Spearman interpretation is Additional Foundation content."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n14\">When commenting on outliers in exam questions, you should: (1) identify the outlier by referring to its approximate value, (2) suggest whether it might be a genuine extreme value or a possible error, and (3) comment on its effect on any averages you have calculated. An outlier pulls the mean towards it but does not affect the median.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n14\">When commenting on outliers in exam questions, you should: (1) identify the outlier by referring to its approximate value, (2) suggest whether it might be a genuine extreme value or a possible error, and (3) comment on its effect on any averages you have calculated. An outlier can pull the mean towards it. The median is usually less affected.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F2: The median can change when an outlier is removed; this happens in the lesson's own example."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "conclusion_html",
+    "find": "<li data-narration-id=\"n39\">Outliers stand far apart from the main cluster, may be genuine extreme values or data errors, and pull the mean towards them without affecting the median.</li>",
+    "replace": "<li data-narration-id=\"n39\">Outliers stand far apart from the main cluster, may be genuine extreme values or data errors, and can pull the mean towards them. The median is usually less affected.</li>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F2: The median can change when an outlier is removed."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "flashcard_questions",
+    "path": "[4].a",
+    "expect": "The mean; outliers pull the mean towards them but do not affect the median.",
+    "value": "The mean; an outlier can pull it towards its value, while the median is usually less affected.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F2: The median can change when an outlier is removed."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 2,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[1].marks",
+    "expect": "Mean = (sum of all values) / 15. Sum = 45+48+50+51+52+53+54+55+56+58+59+60+61+62+95 = 859. Mean = 859/15 = 57.27 (accept 57.3) [1 mark].\nMedian = 8th value when ordered = 55 [1 mark].\nComment (for context): the outlier of 95 pulls the mean upwards (from approximately 55 to 57.3) but does not affect the median.",
+    "value": "Mean = (sum of all values) / 15. Sum = 45+48+50+51+52+53+54+55+56+58+59+60+61+62+95 = 859. Mean = 859/15 = 57.27 (accept 57.3) [1 mark].\nMedian = 8th value when ordered = 55 [1 mark].\nComment (for context): the outlier of 95 pulls the mean upwards (from approximately 54.6 to 57.3) and has much less effect on the median, which changes from 54.5 to 55.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F2: Removing 95 changes the 14-score median to 54.5."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<li data-narration-id=\"n19\"><strong>RPI (Retail Price Index):</strong> Measures changes in the average price of a basket of goods and services purchased by households. Published monthly by the ONS. Used for uprating certain benefits, wages, and rail fares.</li>",
+    "replace": "<li data-narration-id=\"n19\"><strong>RPI (Retail Price Index):</strong> Measures changes in the average price of a basket of goods and services purchased by households. Published monthly by the ONS, it is a legacy measure used for long-term indexation, including index-linked gilts and bonds.</li>",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F3: State-benefit uprating changed from RPI to CPI; use the current ONS description."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "knowledge_checks",
+    "path": "[4].right[0]",
+    "expect": "Changes in the average price of a basket of goods used to uprate wages and certain benefits",
+    "value": "Changes in the average price of a basket of goods and services",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F3: Removes the outdated RPI-uprating claim."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<div class=\"higher-only\">\n  <h2 data-narration-id=\"n23\">Calculating Rates of Change (Higher)</h2>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n24\">At Higher tier, you will be asked to calculate rates of change from tables using a formula that is given in the question. Typical examples include: calculating a birth rate from a table of births and population figures, or calculating a percentage change in house price from year-to-year data.</p>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n25\">The key skill is identifying the right values to substitute into the formula. Read the column headers carefully to identify which values go where. State your working clearly and give your answer in the correct units (rates are usually expressed per 1,000 or as a percentage).</p>\n\n  <h2 data-narration-id=\"n26\">Weighted Index Numbers (Higher)</h2>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n27\">A <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"An index number that gives different items in the basket different weights according to their importance in typical household spending.\">weighted index number</dfn> assigns different weights to different items based on their importance in a typical household&rsquo;s spending. For example, if a family spends 40% of its budget on housing and 5% on entertainment, housing gets a much larger weight in the index. A simple (unweighted) average of all price changes would understate the effect of rising housing costs on the household&rsquo;s total bill.</p>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n28\">The CPI and RPI are both weighted indices. The formula for a weighted index will be given in the question. Your task is to apply it correctly: multiply each item&rsquo;s price relative by its weight, sum these products, and divide by the sum of the weights.</p>\n</div>",
+    "replace": "<h2 data-narration-id=\"n23\">Calculating Rates of Change</h2>\n<p data-narration-id=\"n24\">At Foundation and Higher tier, you may be asked to calculate rates of change from tables using a formula that is given in the question. Typical examples include: calculating a birth rate from a table of births and population figures, or calculating a percentage change in house price from year-to-year data.</p>\n<p data-narration-id=\"n25\">The key skill is identifying the right values to substitute into the formula. Read the column headers carefully to identify which values go where. State your working clearly and give your answer in the correct units (rates are usually expressed per 1,000 or as a percentage).</p>\n\n<div class=\"higher-only\">\n  <h2 data-narration-id=\"n26\">Weighted Index Numbers (Higher)</h2>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n27\">A <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"An index number that gives different items in the basket different weights according to their importance in typical household spending.\">weighted index number</dfn> assigns different weights to different items based on their importance in a typical household&rsquo;s spending. For example, if a family spends 40% of its budget on housing and 5% on entertainment, housing gets a much larger weight in the index. A simple (unweighted) average of all price changes would understate the effect of rising housing costs on the household&rsquo;s total bill.</p>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n28\">The CPI and RPI are both weighted indices. The formula for a weighted index will be given in the question. Your task is to apply it correctly: multiply each item&rsquo;s price relative by its weight, sum these products, and divide by the sum of the weights.</p>\n</div>",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F4: E7b is Additional Foundation content, not Higher-only."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[4].marks",
+    "expect": "Award 1 mark per reason and 1 mark per justification, up to 5 marks.\nMastering: Two reasons, each with a justification linking to a specific feature of time series extrapolation.\n- The past trend may not continue [1]; economic conditions (interest rates, housing policy, global events) can shift the trend direction in ways that are impossible to predict 10 years ahead, so the extrapolated line may diverge significantly from actual prices [1].\n- Seasonal effects compound over time [1]; even small seasonal fluctuations accumulate over 10 years of extrapolation, making point estimates increasingly unreliable as the prediction horizon extends [1].\n- The time series itself may contain outliers or one-off events (e.g. a financial crash) [1]; if such events influenced the trend line used for extrapolation, the forecast inherits these distortions [1].\nSecure: Two reasons, one justified.\nDeveloping: One reason with a justification.\nEmerging: States 'the future is uncertain' without specifying why the time series makes it unreliable.",
+    "value": "Award 1 mark per reason and 1 mark per justification, up to 5 marks.\nMastering: Two reasons, each with a justification linking to a specific feature of time series extrapolation.\n- The past trend may not continue [1]; economic conditions (interest rates, housing policy, global events) can shift the trend direction in ways that are impossible to predict 10 years ahead, so the extrapolated line may diverge significantly from actual prices [1].\n- Seasonal patterns may change [1]; a pattern that has repeated in the past may alter, so a 10-year forecast may not match future prices [1].\n- The time series itself may contain outliers or one-off events (e.g. a financial crash) [1]; if such events influenced the trend line used for extrapolation, the forecast inherits these distortions [1].\nSecure: Two reasons, one justified.\nDeveloping: One reason with a justification.\nEmerging: States 'the future is uncertain' without specifying why the time series makes it unreliable.",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F6: Seasonal effects repeat or change; they do not automatically accumulate."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[5].marks",
+    "expect": "Award up to 6 marks for a balanced discussion with examples and a judgement.\nMastering: Arguments for using rates, acknowledgement of limitations, and a judgement.\n- Comparing total births is misleading because Country B will always have far more births simply because it has 30 times more people [1+1].\n- Birth rates (births per 1,000 population) control for population size, allowing a fairer comparison of the actual fertility patterns in each country [1+1].\n- Rates are more useful for policy purposes: a government wants to know whether the share of new births is declining relative to population size, not just the absolute number [1+1].\n- Limitation: birth rates do not capture age structure differences — a country with a very young population will have a higher birth rate even at the same fertility per woman [1].\n- Judgement: comparing birth rates is generally more meaningful than raw counts when populations differ in size, but further context (age structure, immigration) is still needed for full interpretation [1].\nSecure: Two threads developed.\nDeveloping: One thread with a relevant example.\nEmerging: States 'use a rate' without explaining why.",
+    "value": "Award up to 6 marks for a balanced discussion with examples and a judgement.\nMastering: Arguments for using rates, acknowledgement of limitations, and a judgement.\n- Comparing total births can be misleading because Country B may have more births simply because it has 30 times more people [1+1].\n- Birth rates (births per 1,000 population) control for population size, allowing a fairer comparison of the actual fertility patterns in each country [1+1].\n- Rates are more useful for policy purposes: a government wants to know whether the share of new births is declining relative to population size, not just the absolute number [1+1].\n- Limitation: birth rates do not capture age structure differences — a country with a very young population will have a higher birth rate even at the same fertility per woman [1].\n- Judgement: comparing birth rates is generally more meaningful than raw counts when populations differ in size, but further context (age structure, immigration) is still needed for full interpretation [1].\nSecure: Two threads developed.\nDeveloping: One thread with a relevant example.\nEmerging: States 'use a rate' without explaining why.",
+    "severity": "MEDIUM",
+    "note": "F7: Total births depend on both population and birth rate."
+  }
+]
