+    "find": "<div class=\"higher-only\">\n  <h2 data-narration-id=\"n24\">Interpreting Spearman's Rank and Pearson's PMCC (Higher)</h2>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n25\">At Higher tier, you need to interpret calculated values of two correlation coefficients. <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of the relationship between the rank orders of two variables; denoted r_s.\">Spearman's rank correlation coefficient</dfn> measures the strength and direction of correlation between the rank orders of two variables &mdash; not the raw values. It works for any data that can be ranked, including ordinal data.</p>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n26\"><dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A correlation coefficient that measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables; denoted r.\">Pearson's product moment correlation coefficient</dfn> (PMCC) measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables. The key distinction: Spearman's measures correlation of rank orders, whereas Pearson's measures the linear relationship itself. For a straight-line relationship between continuous variables, Pearson's is more appropriate; for ranked or ordinal data, Spearman's is preferred.</p>\n  <p data-narration-id=\"n27\">The formula for Spearman's rank will always be given in the question &mdash; you do not need to memorise it. What you do need to do is interpret the resulting value using the same thresholds above: \\(|r_s| \\geq 0.6\\) = strong, \\(0.2 \\leq |r_s| < 0.6\\) = weak, \\(|r_s| < 0.2\\) = no correlation.</p>\n</div>",
