+    "expect": "Award up to 6 marks for a balanced discussion with examples and a judgement.\nMastering: Arguments for using rates, acknowledgement of limitations, and a judgement.\n- Comparing total births is misleading because Country B will always have far more births simply because it has 30 times more people [1+1].\n- Birth rates (births per 1,000 population) control for population size, allowing a fairer comparison of the actual fertility patterns in each country [1+1].\n- Rates are more useful for policy purposes: a government wants to know whether the share of new births is declining relative to population size, not just the absolute number [1+1].\n- Limitation: birth rates do not capture age structure differences — a country with a very young population will have a higher birth rate even at the same fertility per woman [1].\n- Judgement: comparing birth rates is generally more meaningful than raw counts when populations differ in size, but further context (age structure, immigration) is still needed for full interpretation [1].\nSecure: Two threads developed.\nDeveloping: One thread with a relevant example.\nEmerging: States 'use a rate' without explaining why.",
